{\*\generator Valtiel Planet Generator Documentation}\viewkind4\uc1
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs36\b Planet Generator\par
\fs22\b0 Editor tool for Unity URP that generates planet & star prefabs at design time.\par
Each planet ships as a self-contained set of cubemap PNGs, materials, and a ready-to-drop prefab.\par
\par
\fs28\b 1. Requirements\par
\fs22\b0
\bullet  Unity 6000.0.61f1 (Unity 6.0 LTS) or later \endash  this is the version the asset was developed and tested on.\par
\bullet  Universal Render Pipeline installed and active.\par
\bullet  For the optional CPU backend: Burst + Collections + Mathematics packages (Package Manager \endash  Unity Registry). These are part of Unity's standard package set; most projects already have them.\par
\par
\fs28\b 2. Opening the tool\par
\fs22\b0 Window \endash  Planet Generator\par
\par
The tool spawns a hidden preview sphere in the active scene. Make sure the scene has a Directional Light for proper preview shading. A ready-to-use demo scene is provided at Samples/Demo.unity.\par
\par
\fs28\b 3. Generator modes\par
\fs22\b0
\bullet  Terrestrial \endash  Continents, oceans, beaches, biomes (desert/grass/forest), mountains, polar caps, clouds.\par
\bullet  Rocky \endash  Airless surface with mare plains, regolith, optional polar ice, and a tileable detail texture mapped triplanar onto the sphere.\par
\bullet  Gas Giant \endash  Latitude bands, zonal flow + curl, storm ovals, pole darkening.\par
\bullet  Star \endash  Photosphere with granulation, sunspots, HDR emission for bloom.\par
\bullet  Debug Noise \endash  Visualizes the underlying 3D fBm.\par
\par
\fs28\b 4. Workflow\par
\fs22\b0 1. Choose mode, tweak sliders \endash  the preview updates live.\par
2. Randomize Seed for variations of the same parameter set.\par
3. Set Name and Export Resolution (1024-2048 recommended).\par
4. Click Save Planet. The tool writes the cubemap PNGs, materials, prefab and params.json under Assets/Procedural Planets Generator/Library/\{Name\}/.\par
5. Drag the prefab into any URP scene. Lit by your scene's directional light and ambient \endash  no setup required.\par
\par
\fs28\b 5. Backend selection (GPU vs CPU)\par
\fs22\b0 The generator ships with two interchangeable backends selectable from the top of the editor window.\par
\par
\bullet  GPU \endash  1-5 ms per regen at 512x512 cubemap. Requires DX11+/Vulkan/Metal/WebGPU. Not WebGL 2.0.\par
\bullet  CPU \endash  50-300 ms per regen at 512x512 cubemap. Works on every platform Unity targets.\par
\par
The CPU path uses Burst-compiled Jobs. Visual output matches the GPU path within float precision. Choose CPU if your dev machine doesn't support compute shaders or for guaranteed cross-platform identical output. Choose GPU otherwise \endash  it's an order of magnitude faster for live slider-tweaking.\par
\par
\fs28\b 6. How the no-pole-pinching works\par
\fs22\b0 Standard equirectangular textures pinch at the poles because the UV parameterization stretches there. Instead, this asset generates cubemaps (6 faces, seamless at edges) and uses a custom URP Lit shader (Valtiel/Planet/Surface) that samples the cubemap by object-space normal rather than UV. Every point on the sphere maps to a unique direction \endash  a unique texel. No pinching, no seams.\par
\par
The shader is otherwise URP Lit-equivalent: main light + additional lights + shadows + GI + fog all work normally.\par
\par
\fs28\b 7. Reproducibility\par
\fs22\b0 params.json contains the seed and every slider value. Re-loading the JSON and re-exporting produces a bit-identical result on the same machine. For cross-machine determinism, ship the exported PNG textures themselves, not just the JSON.\par
\par
